  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Chances for strong storms and flash flooding each day \nthrough Monday.\n\nToday we remain under NW flow aloft along with a stalled boundary \nnear the southern tier of the CWA that has already allowed for \nmorning convection to be tracking along. Daytime heating should \nallow for more scattered development going into this afternoon \nthough CAM guidance is not impressive as coverage could be pretty \nlimited. Nonetheless what does develop has the potential for some \nstrong to damaging winds and very heavy rainfall given decent \nprogged instability and high PWATs. The severe potential is not \nquite as high as previously thought and SPC has removed the Slight \nRisk from our area though keeps much of it in a Marginal Risk.\n\nSunday looks to be a bit greater chance of severe development than \ntoday as bulk shear looks to increase along with a possible \nshortwave traversing the upper flow and a progged increase in \ninstability. We are outlooked with a Slight Risk mainly across the \nnorthern CWA with a Marginal Risk most elsewhere. The threats should \nremain focused on strong to damaging downburst winds and heavy \nrain/localized flash flooding. Another shortwave may allow for some \nenhanced development of convection overnight Sunday as well.\n\nAnother upper disturbance looks to push across the area Monday along \nwith an attendance frontal boundary that will again allow for \ndaytime showers and storms. The stronger shear and instability may \nbe more confined to the southern CWA and SPC keeps the Marginal Risk \nto our southern area as well. Behind this front we will at least see \na pattern shift with a drier airmass and more zonal flow aloft which \nwill allow for a dry midweek. This is relatively shortlived however \nas moisture and potential disturbances allow for shower and storm \nchances back by Thursday and Friday.",
